{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== 10 OF 20 CHANGES ARE MERGED TO MAIN. 8229 closed. a49e implemented and pushed. ===\n\n*** THE FIRST HALF OF THIS EPIC IS ON MAIN. *** Submitted Gerrit 1090, which carried its 9\nancestors: 1012 (7468/S1), 1014 (f184/S2), 1023 (dc0d/S4), 1032 (2682), 1043 (2932/S3),\n1048 (9249/S7), 1067 (281f), 1081 (3a98), 1086 (343b/S5), 1090 (1903). All 10 MERGED.\n\nTHE REGRESSION CHECK THAT LICENSED IT (the operator's authorization is conditional on NO\nregression, and explicitly says passing CI is NOT sufficient):\n1. Rebase of the 10 onto current main: CLEAN, 0 conflicts.\n2. Full suite on main+1-10: 8372 passed, 0 FAILED, 12 errors. THE 12 ERRORS ARE NOT OURS -- a\n   control run of the same 3 test files on PLAIN origin/main reproduces all 12 identically\n   (39 passed / 12 errors, same test ids). They are a probe artifact: I ran via PYTHONPATH in an\n   un-provisioned worktree, so the packaging tests build a wheel and trip the REPO_ROOT\n   leak-guard. Delta attributable to the 10 commits: ZERO. RUN THE CONTROL BEFORE CALLING\n   ANYTHING A REGRESSION -- a failing probe is as misleading as a passing one.\n3. LLM BEHAVIOUR ON THIS PROJECT'S OWN CONFIG (we dogfood these gates, so this is the one that\n   matters). We run BARE claude-opus-4-8 (Pass-1) and claude-sonnet-4-6 (verifier) over direct\n   Anthropic; at call time the strings are provider-qualified. Diffed main vs main+1-10:\n     anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6  temp sent, anthropic cache  -> IDENTICAL both sides\n     anthropic:claude-opus-4-8    temp sent -> temp WITHDRAWN  -> WIRE-IDENTICAL, see below\n     bare strings                 no cache                     -> IDENTICAL both sides\n   The opus delta is a NO-OP on the wire: capabilities.py's override table documents, from a\n   PRODUCTION observation on the code-review bot, that pydantic-ai's Anthropic adapter already\n   drops temperature for this model (models/anthropic.py:641) -- so main SENDS it and the SDK\n   DISCARDS it. Withdrawing it explicitly is wire-identical, minus the per-call warning and minus\n   the false belief that greedy decoding is in effect. Greedy Pass-2 runs on SONNET, which keeps\n   temperature, so verifier determinism is preserved. cfg.temperature is None here anyway.\nRESIDUAL RISKS I DID NOT WAVE AWAY: (a) 281f removes REBAR_LLM_BEDROCK_MODEL_ID; unknown config\nkeys normally WARN, but REBAR_CONFIG_UNKNOWN_KEYS=error promotes them to a HARD ERROR\n(_config_schema.py:60), so a deployment that sets that key AND runs strict fails at startup;\n(b) 343b lands PARTIAL -- provenance reaches the runner payload but finalize_verdict\n(plan_review/orchestrator.py:528) still does not receive it. Additive, not regressive.\n\nA LIVE CORRECTNESS GAP THIS TURNED UP, INDEPENDENT OF ANYTHING WE LANDED: code-review's Pass-2\n`temperature: 0` HAS NEVER TAKEN EFFECT. Code review has no verifier downgrade, so ALL its passes\nrun on opus, whose temperature the SDK silently drops. The gate reviewing this epic has never had\nthe greedy determinism its config claims. Worth its own ticket.\n\n=== 8229 CLOSED (took THREE close attempts; both failures were instructive) ===\n1. The completion verifier REFUSED my \"greppable commit message\" AC: it has repository FILE tools\n   and CANNOT READ GIT HISTORY. I had written that AC two rounds earlier to give the original an\n   oracle -- but I gave it one a HUMAN can run, not one THIS GATE can. The two gates have\n   different capabilities and satisfying one does not satisfy the other.\n   docs/plan-review-gate.md describes this exact class and its advisory lint, warning such an AC\n   \"makes the completion verifier hunt for code proof that cannot exist and burn a close-gate\n   cycle\". FIX: tag it [operator-attested]. WHY THE LINT DID NOT CATCH IT: naming a proving\n   command trips the lint's deliberate codebase-verifiable SUPPRESSION co-signal --\n   `git log ... | grep ...` READS as in-repo but git history is not codebase-verifiable. That\n   looks like a genuine blind spot in an otherwise precision-first lint.\n2. Tagging the AC then tripped the PLAN-REVIEW close gate: stale-material. So FIXING AN AC AT\n   CLOSE TIME COSTS A FULL PLAN-REVIEW ROUND (a 5th, for 8229). That is exactly the cost the\n   plan-time lint exists to avoid.\nFinal: closed on change 1132 (Verified +1). Its LLM-Review had earlier been a fail-closed INFRA\nveto -- \"[LLM-Review: BLOCK - coverage-gap (review-error)]\", explicitly \"infrastructure, not your\ncode\" -- re-triggered and passed. Per docs/review-policy.md any coverage-gap is a maintainer\nre-trigger, NOT a code fix; do not amend for one.\n\n=== a49e IMPLEMENTED AND PUSHED as change 1140 (commit b4b475b04) ===\nrunner.py 647 -> 647 (LOC-NEUTRAL, as planned: it swaps construct-plus-finally for a `with`).\n`grep -c 'with ProviderSession' runner.py` is now 1; the redundant explicit close is gone.\nFull suite 8258 passed / 74 skipped / 0 FAILED; lint 0; typecheck 0.\nHeld-out TDD: 5 tests; implementer saw ONE. The 2 defect tests were RED first for the RIGHT\nreasons and are green now; the 3 invariant guards were green throughout and had to stay so.\nMutation: re-adding the redundant close fails the exactly-once test with \"closed 2 times\".\nITS PLAN'S CITATIONS HAD ALL ROTTED and I re-measured them: construction 301->294, guard try\n474->445, finally 582->501, providers.py dunders 295-299 -> 323/326, assert_gated 256->261.\ne6bd and 8229 shifted the file under the plan. A plan citing file:line in a file its own story is\nrewriting WILL go stale; the plan now says re-locate by CONTENT.\nI also fixed a vacuity hole ruff caught (B017): `pytest.raises(Exception)` in the leak test would\nhave passed on ANY exception. Now pinned to the injected RuntimeError with a match.\n\n=== HOW TO REBASE THE REMAINDER (diagnosed; do NOT do it yet) ===\nA plain `git rebase origin/main` now fails EARLY at commit 5 (2932) with ADD/ADD conflicts on\ntest_bedrock_provider.py and capabilities.py. That is OUR OWN LANDED WORK COLLIDING WITH ITSELF:\nGerrit's server-side rebase gave the merged commits new SHAs, so git cannot patch-id-match them\nand replays our originals on top. DO NOT resolve those.\nCORRECT COMMAND: `git rebase --onto origin/main 96d683a0d` (96d683a0d = 1903 = the last landed\ncommit), which replays ONLY the un-landed ones. That reduces it to EXACTLY ONE conflict:\ngate_dispatch.py in commit 1484, 3 hunks, the largest ~49 lines. Genuine semantic clash -- 1484\nextracts 320 lines into plan_review_recovery.py while main's 2c89 independently cut 252 from the\nsame file; 2c89's new \"cancel a mid-run review on own-plan edits\" behaviour must be carried INTO\nthe extracted module, not dropped.\nDEFERRED ON PURPOSE: resolving forces new patchsets on all 10 remaining changes, dropping every\nvote INCLUDING the operator's manual Verified overrides on 1121/1122. With a49e, 172e's gap,\n7761, d23e and 343b still open, main will move again first -- so do ONE rebase at the end,\nimmediately before landing, not now.\n\n=== 172e IS STILL BLOCKED BY A REAL FUNCTIONAL GAP (verified in source, not a test gap) ===\nconfig.py:362 `resolve_model` is `return step or workflow or cfg.model` with NO call to\nresolve_model_string, and workflow/gates/code-review.yaml:143-155 (the verify step) has NO\n`model:` key at all. So code-review's Pass-2 verifier resolves cfg.model -- the FRONTIER model --\nand adding `model: standard` to the YAML would pass the LITERAL STRING \"standard\". Both of 172e's\nremaining ACs name exactly this. It is in scope and unfinished. NOTE the overlap: 7761 also\nconsumes resolve_model_string, and d23e is blocked by 172e + 7761."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785535548804564001,"uuid":"66ce1ed9-511b-49a9-8595-066e1723f720"}